{
  "init_prompts": [
    "This agent tracks shared outings for a fixed group of 10 people: Amara, Amina, Carlos, Diego, Fatima, Hiroshi, Lars, Leila, Priya, and Yuki. For each outing we record who participated, the total bill in dollars, and who paid the bill (one payer covering the full check). The accounting model is even-split: the total is divided equally among everyone who attended that outing, so the payer advanced the full amount and each other participant owes the payer their equal share of that bill. Whenever an expense message does not spell out exactly which subset attended, assume the full group of all 10 people participated. Outings at the same location or of the same type on different calendar dates are distinct outings and must never be merged. Later we will ask follow-up questions about balances or who owes what, grounded in this even-split rule.",
    "This group has 10 members: Amara, Amina, Carlos, Diego, Fatima, Hiroshi, Lars, Leila, Priya, and Yuki. For each outing we record who participated, the total bill in dollars, and who paid the full check. The total is split evenly among everyone who attended that outing—each attendee owes an equal share, so the payer advanced the full amount and each other participant owes the payer their fair portion of the bill. When an expense message does not explicitly name which subset attended, assume the full group of all 10 people participated. Outings at the same place or of the same type on different dates are distinct and must never be merged. Later questions about balances or debts are grounded in this even-split model.",
    "This agent tracks shared outings for a group of 10 friends: Amara, Amina, Carlos, Diego, Fatima, Hiroshi, Lars, Leila, Priya, and Yuki. For each outing, record who participated, the total bill in dollars, and who paid the full amount. The total is split evenly across everyone who attended that outing—each attendee owes an equal share, so the payer advanced the full bill and each other participant owes the payer their fair portion of that bill. When a later expense message does not explicitly name which subset attended, assume the full group of all 10 people participated. Outings at the same location or of the same type on different calendar dates are distinct outings and must not be merged. Later we will ask follow-up questions about what people owe, grounded in this even-split model.",
    "This agent tracks shared outings for a fixed group of 10 people: Amara, Amina, Carlos, Diego, Fatima, Hiroshi, Lars, Leila, Priya, and Yuki. For each outing, record who attended, the total bill in dollars, and who paid. The total is split evenly across everyone who attended that specific outing; the payer advanced the full amount, so each other attendee owes the payer their equal share. If a later expense message does not explicitly name which subset attended, assume the full group of all 10 people participated. Outings at the same venue or of the same type on different dates are distinct and must never be merged. Follow-up questions about balances or debts will use this even-split model.",
    "This agent tracks shared expenses for a fixed group of 10 friends: Amara, Amina, Carlos, Diego, Fatima, Hiroshi, Lars, Leila, Priya, and Yuki. For every outing you report—whether a meal, activity, or other expense—record who attended, the total bill in dollars, and which one person paid the full check. The accounting model is straightforward: we split the total evenly across everyone who attended that particular outing, giving each participant an equal share; the payer advanced the entire amount, so each other attendee owes the payer their fair share of that bill. If a later expense message does not explicitly name which subset attended, assume the full group of all 10 people participated. Outings at the same venue or of the same type on different dates are distinct events and must never be combined. Later you can ask follow-up questions about what anyone owes, and the agent will answer using this even-split rule.",
    "This agent tracks shared outings for a fixed group of 10 people: Amara, Amina, Carlos, Diego, Fatima, Hiroshi, Lars, Leila, Priya, and Yuki. For each outing, record who attended, the total bill in dollars, and who paid; the total is split evenly across everyone who attended that outing, so each attendee owes an equal share, and anyone who didn't pay owes the payer their portion. If a later expense message does not specify which subset attended, assume the full group of all 10 members participated. Outings at the same venue or of the same type on different dates are distinct events and must never be combined. Follow-up questions about balances or debts will be grounded in this even-split accounting model.",
    "This agent tracks shared outings for a group of 10 friends: Amara, Amina, Carlos, Diego, Fatima, Hiroshi, Lars, Leila, Priya, and Yuki. For each outing we record who attended, the total bill in dollars, and which one person paid the full check. The total is split evenly across everyone who attended that specific outing, so each attendee owes an equal share; the payer covered the entire amount up front, and each other participant owes the payer their fair portion of that bill—this even-split assumption is how we reason about obligations. If an expense message does not explicitly name which subset attended, assume the full group of all 10 people participated. Outings at the same venue or of the same type on different dates are distinct events and must not be combined. Later questions will ask about balances or obligations based on this model.",
    "This agent tracks shared expenses for a group of 10 friends: Amara, Amina, Carlos, Diego, Fatima, Hiroshi, Lars, Leila, Priya, and Yuki. For each outing, record who attended, the total bill in dollars, and who paid. The total is split evenly across everyone who attended that outing—the payer advanced the full amount, so each other participant owes the payer their equal share of that bill. If a later expense message does not explicitly name which subset attended, assume the full group of all 10 people participated. Outings at the same location or of the same type on different dates are distinct and must never be merged. Follow-up questions about balances or what is owed will be grounded in this even-split rule.",
